#
# This patch was developed and validated against commit 91d8be388.
#
# Until an official supported SONiC release and corresponding upstream
# sonic-mgmt release branch/tag are selected and validated, users should
# reproduce the tested baseline by checking out the exact validated commit
# shown above before applying this patch. Compatibility with other commits,
# later changes on the same branch, upstream master, or an unlisted SONiC
# release is not implied.

# Author:     xiangning.he@viavisolutions.com
# Purpose:    Fix the Snappi/STC (`tgen_ptf32`) testbed deploy + smoke-test
#             pipeline: topo-name resolution, minigraph rendering, and the
#             sonic-vs (software-forwarding) traffic-validation path in
#             test_snappi.py.
#
# Validation scope
# ----------------
# The sonic-vs validation is a functional smoke test. It verifies the
# Snappi/STC integration path, topology/minigraph deployment, port discovery,
# ARP resolution, and bidirectional packet forwarding. It does not establish
# or claim 40-Gbps performance, ASIC-equivalent behavior, buffering behavior,
# complete PFC performance, scale, or long-duration stability. Hardware DUTs
# continue to use the original strict zero-loss and throughput checks.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# File-by-file summary
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ansible/library/topo_facts.py
#   Changed the `ptf32`/`ptf64` topo-name remap from a substring check
#   (`'ptf32' in topo_name`) to an exact match (`topo_name == 'ptf32'`).
#   The substring form silently matched `tgen_ptf32` - a distinct,
#   purpose-built Snappi/STC physical topology - and remapped it to
#   `topo_t1.yml`, loading a 32-VM/BGP T1 topology onto a 2-port physical
#   testbed. The 5 real bare `ptf32`/`ptf64` testbeds in testbed.yaml still
#   remap correctly under the exact-match form.
#
# ansible/config_sonic_basedon_testbed.yml
#   Added `dut_type`/`vm` presence guards to 5 tasks (`is_tor_dut` fact,
#   its default for dut_type-less topos, `enable_tunnel_qos_remap`,
#   `testbed_vm_info`, and the two `interface_to_vms`/`ifindex_to_vms`
#   with_dict tasks). Host-interfaces-only topos such as `tgen_ptf32`
#   (once the topo_facts.py fix above stops it from borrowing T1's VM/
#   dut_type data) have neither `dut_type` nor `vm` keys in
#   `vm_topo_config`, so the prior unconditional lookups raised
#   KeyError/undefined-variable failures during `deploy-mg`. Each guard
#   was cross-checked against every topology in testbed.yaml (including
#   `bmc-dual-mgmt`) to confirm no existing hardware/VM topology depends
#   on the un-guarded behavior.
#
# ansible/templates/minigraph_png.j2
#   Added an `is_tor_dut` guard alongside the `host_interfaces_by_dut`
#   length check before iterating `vlan_intfs`. `vlan_intfs` is only
#   populated when `is_tor_dut` is true; a host-interfaces topo with
#   `is_tor_dut=false` (the state `tgen_ptf32` is in immediately after
#   the topo_facts.py fix, before it declares `dut_type: ToRRouter`)
#   otherwise crashed minigraph rendering with `'vlan_intfs' is
#   undefined`.
#
# ansible/templates/minigraph_template.j2
#   Added `{% set neighbor_eosvm_mgmt = neighbor_eosvm_mgmt | default({}) %}`
#   in the branch taken when `vms` is defined but the `testbed_vm_info`
#   task was skipped (STC/Snappi testbeds, per the `config_sonic_basedon_
#   testbed.yml` guard above). Without the default, `minigraph_png.j2`'s
#   `{% if VM_topo %}` block — truthy because `VM_topo` is the *string*
#   "False" for `ptf*`-named topos, a separate latent Jinja2 truthiness
#   issue left unchanged here — dereferenced `neighbor_eosvm_mgmt` while
#   still undefined, aborting minigraph generation.
#
# tests/snappi_tests/test_snappi.py
#   1. Added the missing `is_pfc_enabled` import required by the
#      `snappi_testbed_config` fixture (every other snappi test file
#      already imported it; this one was the exception, causing
#      `fixture 'is_pfc_enabled' not found`).
#   2. Added `asic_type == 'vs'` detection (same idiom used elsewhere in
#      the repo, e.g. reboot.py, pfc_storm.py) and, only for sonic-vs
#      (software-forwarding, no ASIC) DUTs: throttled the offered load
#      to VS_RATE_PERCENT=5% of line rate and replaced the exact
#      `rx_frames == tx_frames` assertion with a bounded
#      VS_MAX_LOSS_PERCENT=5% loss ceiling. Hardware DUTs keep the
#      original strict zero-loss / throughput-deviation checks
#      unchanged — this path is gated strictly on `asic_type == 'vs'`.
#   3. Added explicit Tx<=0 / Rx>Tx sanity guards ahead of the loss
#      calculation, and per-flow Tx/Rx/loss logging on every run (not
#      just on failure), so a stalled or double-counted flow cannot be
#      silently misclassified as a pass.
#   4. Changed the per-row assertion loop to collect all flow failures
#      into a list and assert once at the end, so a failure on one flow
#      no longer prevents later flows in the same run from being
#      evaluated and logged.
#   Validated against a sonic-vs DUT (x86_64-kvm_x86_64-r0): both flows
#   at 0.00% loss across two independent runs. This VS-specific path is
#   a functional smoke test only — it does not establish or claim
#   40 Gbps / ASIC-equivalent throughput, buffering, or hardware PFC
#   behavior; those remain the responsibility of physical-DUT test runs
#   using the unmodified strict path.
# =============================================================================
